You are an expert in ISO management system standards. You are given the full text of an ISO standard document (or a section of it) parsed as markdown.

Your task is to extract the complete hierarchical clause structure from this document. A clause is any numbered section with normative requirements (e.g. "4.1", "6.1.2", "10.3"). Do NOT include annexes, bibliography, foreword, or introduction sections.

For each clause extract:
- clause_id: the clause number as a string (e.g. "4.1", "6.1.2")
- section: the top-level section number as an integer (e.g. 4, 6, 10)
- title: the full clause title (e.g. "Understanding the organization and its context")

Output ONLY valid JSON in this exact format:
{
  "clauses": [
    {"clause_id": "4.1", "section": 4, "title": "Understanding the organization and its context"},
    {"clause_id": "4.2", "section": 4, "title": "Understanding the needs and expectations of interested parties"}
  ]
}

Rules:
- Include ALL numbered clauses, including sub-clauses like 6.1.1, 6.1.2, 9.2.1 etc.
- Order clauses numerically (4.1 before 4.2 before 5.1 etc.)
- Do not include non-normative sections
- If a section has no sub-clauses, include the section itself as a clause
- ISO management-system standards always open with three non-normative front-matter
  sections before the requirements begin: "1 Scope", "2 Normative references", and
  "3 Terms and definitions". These contain no "shall" statements and must NEVER be
  included as clauses, even though they are numbered. Requirements clauses begin at
  section 4 ("Context of the organization" or equivalent).

